                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of countermelody."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "713381a0-3574-44ce-8a13-65b763ba96d7",
                                                "sense_id": "5d71ef66-5f48-3173-afd1-60617ab78b6d",
                                                "text": "The accompanying texture, too, is rather more than a simple filling in of the harmony. In the first phrase, for example, the beginning of the counter-melody in the Sextus part is shadowed in shorter note values in the tenor of Choir 1 and the alto of Choir 2, while the rising scale of the plainsong (bars 2-3 (Bt 3-5)) is shadowed by the bass of Choir 2."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "21f28425-f72b-4f2f-a0e8-d63f113c26ab",
                                                "sense_id": "5d71ef66-5f48-3173-afd1-60617ab78b6d",
                                                "text": "This section has compositional elements including melody, counter-melody, sustained harmony, rhythmic harmonic accompaniment, bass line and rhythmic accompaniment, but they are all marked mf."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "067bc27b-89ac-4608-88e4-ee3f31553a74",
                                                "sense_id": "5d71ef66-5f48-3173-afd1-60617ab78b6d",
                                                "text": "The first violin played the tune throughout, the second violin and the viola played after-beats (the pahs), the cello had some kind of sustained counter-melody or doubled the bass, who played the ooms."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
